For `File.WriteAllText`, `File.ReadAllText`, and `Directory.Exists`, the best approach is to adopt the **`System.IO.Abstractions`** NuGet package — it's battle-tested and already wraps the entire `System.IO` surface.

## Setup

**1. Install packages:**
```bash
dotnet add <YourProject> package System.IO.Abstractions
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

**2. Register in DI** (Program.cs / Startup.cs):
```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

**3. Inject `IFileSystem`** instead of calling statics directly:
```csharp
public class ConfigManager(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string Load(string path)
        => fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);

    public void Save(string path, string content)
        => fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, content);

    public bool EnsureDirectory(string path)
        => fileSystem.Directory.Exists(path);
}
```

**4. Test with `MockFileSystem`:**
```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/config.json", new MockFileData("{\"key\": \"value\"}") }
});
mockFs.AddDirectory("/data");

var manager = new ConfigManager(mockFs);

Assert.Equal("{\"key\": \"value\"}", manager.Load("/config.json"));
Assert.True(manager.EnsureDirectory("/data"));

manager.Save("/out.txt", "hello");
Assert.Equal("hello", mockFs.File.ReadAllText("/out.txt"));
```

## Why this over a custom wrapper?

| | Custom `IFileWrapper` | `System.IO.Abstractions` |
|---|---|---|
| Coverage | Only methods you wrap | Full `System.IO` surface |
| Maintenance | You own it | Community-maintained |
| Test helpers | You build them | `MockFileSystem` included |
| Risk of bugs | Higher | Battle-tested |

**Key rule:** only wrap what you actually call. `IFileSystem` gives you `File`, `Directory`, `Path`, etc. as properties — you just replace the static calls with the injected instance and your code becomes fully testable.

